t task for them doesn't make practical sense—the contract would be empty. Since the constraint seems intended for shared pkg/ packages and the examples only show contracts for those, I'll move forward with implementation tasks for cmd/printenv and cmd/id without contracts, and focus on rel06.1 as the earliest release with ready requirements.\n\nNow I'm checking the requirement_states to see if there's anything earlier I'm missing. Most utilities in rel02.0 through rel05.3 are already implemented, and the ones marked \"not started\" with ready requirements start at rel06.1. Let me verify this is actually the earliest—srd004-ts shows ready requirements but ts is in rel15.0, so there's a mismatch I need to clarify.\n\nI should focus on earlier releases first. Since rel06.1 has printenv and id with ready requirements, I'll structure my task proposals around those, breaking printenv into three tasks that cover the core implementation, continuation, and finishing touches.\n\nFor the id utility, I'll start a separate task since it's independent. Now I need to think through what these requirements actually mean—printenv is straightforward: it prints all environment variables when called without arguments, a specific variable when given one, and supports null-delimited output with the `-0` or `--null` flags, plus standard help and version options. For srd040-printenv with groups R1 (3 reqs), R2 (4 reqs), R3 (3 reqs):\n- R1 probably covers: basic functionality, argument parsing, exit codes\n- R2 probably covers: flag options (-0/--null), multiple variable args, version/help\n- R3 probably covers: error handling, edge cases, differential testing\n\nFor srd041-id:\n- id prints user/group IDs\n- Flags: -u (user), -g (group), -G (groups), -n (name), -r (real), -Z (SELinux context)\n- Groups R1 (3 reqs), R2 (4 reqs), R3 (3 reqs), R4 (3 reqs) = 13 requirements\n\nLooking at the git log, I can see the stitch pipeline uses a consistent naming pattern: `[stitch] srdNNN-name RX.Y, RX.Y