cribe what each requirement accomplishes. Based on the roadmap description of printenv's core functionality, I'm mapping out the likely requirement structure: basic variable printing when no arguments are given, retrieving specific variables when names are provided, proper exit codes, NUL-delimited output support, and handling edge cases like nonexistent variables. I see the pattern now—the stitch agent will read the SRD file for the actual details, so I should write high-level requirement descriptions that reference the SRD sections rather than trying to guess the specifics. Let me structure the requirements to match that approach, pointing to the relevant SRD sections for R1.1, R1.2, and the other requirements. I'm working through the constraint that each SRD requirement needs its own task requirement entry. I initially tried to group related requirements together, but that violates the one-to-one mapping rule. So I need to create separate entries for R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R2.1, each referencing the SRD specification directly without trying to infer what each sub-requirement covers.\n\nFor acceptance criteria, I should focus on concrete, checkable outcomes like successful builds, passing linters, and differential tests against a reference binary if available. I'm also thinking about whether to scaffold the test file in the first task or defer it to later tasks—scaffolding it early makes sense so subsequent tasks can add test cases incrementally.\n\nNow I'm writing out the final output with all the task definitions and their dependencies.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the task split. Printenv is such a minimal utility—the GNU source is barely 100 lines of C, so a Go implementation would be 50-80 lines. Breaking it into three tasks feels excessive, but the constraint of max 4 sub-requirements per task means I need at least 3 tasks to cover all 10 requirements. I could combine implementation and testing in a single task, but the requirements are clearly separated, so I'll stick with the three-task structure.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the final YAML. I need to make sure each task description points the stitch agent to the right SRD requirements and specifies which files to create or modify, plus any reference binaries for differential testing and dependencies on existing pkg/ code. The CLAUDE.md already covers the patterns for differential testing and signal handling, so I just need to be specific about the requirements and files involved. I'm verifying the file structure for each command—printenv and id both follow the pattern of main.go and a corresponding test file. I need to decide whether each task creates or modifies the test file, since the first task establishes the initial test cases while subsequent tasks expand them alongside the implementation.\n\nOne more consideration: the reference binaries should use Homebrew's g-prefix convention (gprintenv and gid), which aligns with the project's testing pattern for coreutils implementations.\n\nI also need to remember that any utility writing to stdout requires calling sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main, per the CLAUDE.md guidelines.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the complete task structure with weight verification to ensure all requirements are properly distributed across the four tasks.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52415,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52415},"output_tokens":31,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8708bb7-ec50-4388-b895-04962eba6898","uuid":"65b73b25-f785-42f3-ade3-74f49625282a"}
